**Lemma 3.2 (kernel condition with arbitrary inaccessible multiplicity).**
In either regime one actual low live-bearing label has a two-dimensional
fibre and one coefficient ray TA.11. Every supported later-failure
effect at that label annihilates

$$
k_K=\left(1,-\frac{b_Kc_1}{h_1c_0},
                 -\frac{d_Kc_2}{h_2c_0}\right)^T,
\qquad\ker K(c)=\mathbb Ck_K.
\tag{TA.18}
$$

Consequently a positive multiple of $B_\epsilon$ can be attached only if
$b_\epsilon k_K=0$. For nonzero coordinates $c_1,c_2$ this is

$$
\frac{c_2}{c_1}=t_\epsilon,
\qquad
t_\epsilon=-\frac{b_Kh_2(b_\epsilon)_1}
                   {d_Kh_1(b_\epsilon)_2}.
\tag{TA.19}
$$

Neither coordinate may vanish alone in this equation. The kernel
condition is also sufficient for adjoining that spectral failure to a
given single-ray live map by one full TP decoder, as constructed in §8.

Proof. The matrix of $K(c)$ is

$$
\begin{pmatrix}
c_0h_0&c_1a_K&c_2c_K\\
c_1b_K&c_0h_1&0\\c_2d_K&0&c_0h_2
\end{pmatrix}.
\tag{TA.20}
$$

Its determinant is $h_0h_1h_2c_0(c_0^2-\theta c_1^2-\omega c_2^2)=0$,
whereas its lower-right minor is $c_0^2h_1h_2\ne0$. Its rank is two.
For completeness, the common-decoder proof underlying RH Lemma 8.2
also applies to the strong real-null products. Write supported encoder
matrices $C_j:\mathbb C^3\to\mathbb C^d$ and compressed live-decoder
matrices $R_i:\mathbb C^d\to\mathbb C^3$. A nonzero product has rank
two, excluding $d=1$. For $d=2$, choose a nonzero $R_{i_0}C_{j_0}$.
Then $R_{i_0}$ is injective and $C_{j_0}$ is onto. Every nonzero
$C_j$ gives a rank-two $R_{i_0}C_j$ with the same image. The left
null line of TA.20 is

$$
\left(1,-\frac{a_Kc_1}{h_1c_0},
          -\frac{c_Kc_2}{h_2c_0}\right)^T.
\tag{TA.21}
$$

All its fixed amplitudes are nonzero, so the image fixes the coefficient
ray. Injectivity therefore makes all nonzero $C_j$ proportional to
$C_{j_0}$. For any nonzero $R_i$, surjectivity makes $R_iC_{j_0}$
nonzero. Its right kernel is $\ker C_{j_0}$; TA.18 fixes the same
coefficient ray. This proves one ray for the whole actual branch,
without assuming a single encoder Kraus matrix or an isometric decoder.

Every encoder matrix kills $k_K$. Every later effect obtained using
that same decoder is a sum $\sum_jC_j^\dagger D_fC_j$, where $D_f$
is the decoder's total failure effect, so it kills $k_K$ too. TA.19
is its explicit scalar equation. Both row components in TA.15 are
nonzero, so if either $c_1$ or $c_2$ vanishes, the equation forces
both to vanish, which contradicts TA.11. The argument applies to a
coarse CP branch with unacquired integral multiplicity through its
finite-dimensional Kraus representation, and almost everywhere in the
admitted measurable class. $\square$

